---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import {
  APPLICATION_URL,
  PRE_INTEREST_URL,
  QUOTE_URL,
} from "../lib/urls";

const title = "Premarital asset protection — drafts, not a law firm";
const description =
  "Start a bounded software quote, register pre-interest, or submit an application at user.pmap.pro. PMAP produces educational planning drafts, not legal advice.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="fine">Educational product · same property rules for every marriage</p>
    <h1>Work out what you actually own — then have a lawyer write the real instruments.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Answer questions, branch like a choose-your-own-adventure, and get a structured pack:
      a disclosure schedule, a prenuptial draft to read rather than sign, a memo written for
      your attorney, and a list of the things no agreement can do at all.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href={QUOTE_URL}>Get a planning quote</a>
      <a class="button" href={PRE_INTEREST_URL}>Register pre-interest</a>
      <a class="button" href={APPLICATION_URL}>Submit an application</a>
      <a class="button" href="/how/">How it works</a>
    </div>
    <p class="fine">
      Customer pages live at user.pmap.pro. Public submissions go only to api.pmap.pro.
      app.pmap.pro is a compatibility redirect, not a second application authority.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="intake-heading">
    <h2 id="intake-heading">Choose the smallest next step</h2>
    <div class="intake-grid">
      <article class="intake-card">
        <h3>Quote</h3>
        <p>
          Share a jurisdiction, broad asset band, timeline, and the coordination
          services you are considering. The result is a bounded software and
          coordination estimate, not a legal-fee quote.
        </p>
        <a href={QUOTE_URL}>Start the quote</a>
      </article>
      <article class="intake-card">
        <h3>Pre-interest</h3>
        <p>
          Leave only contact information and service interests. Contact consent
          is required, and registration does not create an attorney-client
          relationship or reserve representation.
        </p>
        <a href={PRE_INTEREST_URL}>Register pre-interest</a>
      </article>
      <article class="intake-card">
        <h3>Application</h3>
        <p>
          Submit high-level planning context for human review. Do not upload or
          paste identity numbers, account details, credentials, private keys,
          one-time passwords, or full financial documents.
        </p>
        <a href={APPLICATION_URL}>Open the application</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul class="points">
      <li><strong>A disclosure schedule.</strong> Across every forum we cover, whether each party knew what the other owned is one of the two facts that decide whether an agreement survives. This is that exhibit.</li>
      <li><strong>A memo for your attorney.</strong> Your matter summary, the issues your answers raise, the execution formalities your forums require, and open questions — each legal proposition cited so counsel can check it or throw it out.</li>
      <li><strong>A prenuptial draft to read, not to sign.</strong> It exists so you can tell whether the agreement you receive is complete.</li>
      <li><strong>A post-wedding checklist.</strong> Several parts of a premarital plan only work if they are done <em>after</em> the wedding. That list is where plans usually fail.</li>
      <li><strong>The gaps in your own answers</strong>, worst first, with the fix for each.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Divorce on the horizon: preserve ownership before you improvise</h2>
    <p>
      Once separation or divorce is reasonably foreseeable, the safest planning work is usually not a
      last-minute shuffle. It is proving what you already own, keeping separate property separate,
      documenting legitimate transactions, and getting jurisdiction-specific advice before changing title.
    </p>
    <ul class="points">
      <li><strong>Trace separate property.</strong> Preserve purchase records, deeds, account statements, inheritance records, gift letters, tax records, and evidence showing where the money came from.</li>
      <li><strong>Stop accidental commingling.</strong> Do not casually mix separately owned funds with joint accounts or use joint funds to pay expenses on an asset you intend to claim as separate without understanding the consequences.</li>
      <li><strong>Document real loans and real gifts as what they actually are.</strong> A bona fide loan should have a written obligation and ordinary repayment terms. A genuine gift should actually transfer ownership. A fake “gift” or “loan” that is secretly meant to come back later can be treated very differently.</li>
      <li><strong>Review trusts, entities, and exemptions early.</strong> Properly formed trusts, companies, retirement accounts, homestead protections, and other exempt assets may matter, but their effect depends heavily on timing, control, funding source, and local law.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not assume changing the name on title changes the marital-property answer.</strong> Courts often look at source of funds, timing, intent, control, consideration, and the parties' conduct — not just whose name appears on the document.</li>
      <li><strong>Get advice before any transfer to a friend or relative.</strong> A genuine transfer may be valid, but if the understanding is “hold this for me and give it back after the divorce,” that fact pattern can look like a nominee arrangement or sham transfer and may be unwound or sanctioned.</li>
      <li><strong>Never lie in disclosure.</strong> If a court, statute, discovery request, financial affidavit, or agreement requires disclosure, disclose the asset and the transaction accurately.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      The key distinction is substance over labels: lawful planning changes ownership for real and complies
      with disclosure duties. Concealment, backdating, sham transfers, and false statements are not asset protection.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>“This house is mine. This car is mine. This dog is mine.”</h2>
    <p>
      A strong agreement can identify specific property and state the parties' intended ownership with as much
      precision as possible. The useful drafting pattern is not merely “mine forever”; it is an asset-by-asset
      declaration tied to title, source of funds, debt, appreciation, income, maintenance, insurance, taxes,
      replacement property, and what happens if the asset is sold or refinanced.
    </p>
    <ul class="points">
      <li><strong>House:</strong> identify the property by legal description or address, state who owns the equity, who is responsible for the mortgage and carrying costs, and how later renovations or principal payments affect any claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Car:</strong> identify the vehicle, title owner, loan responsibility, insurance, maintenance, replacement proceeds, and whether a later trade-in stays separate.</li>
      <li><strong>Pet:</strong> state ownership, registration and microchip information, veterinary responsibility, possession expectations, and what happens on separation, subject to local law.</li>
      <li><strong>Business:</strong> state who owns the existing equity, what happens to later appreciation, whether marital labor creates any claim, and how distributions or reinvested earnings are treated.</li>
      <li><strong>Accounts and investments:</strong> identify the account, the premarital balance, later contributions, gains, dividends, tax treatment, and whether proceeds remain separate when moved to a replacement account.</li>
      <li><strong>Inherited or gifted property:</strong> record the source, date, donor or estate, and the parties' intent that the property and traceable replacements remain separately owned.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A practical clause family is: <em>“The parties acknowledge that Asset X is and shall remain the separate
      property of Party A, together with its traceable proceeds, substitutions, replacements, and appreciation,
      except to the extent the parties later agree otherwise in a signed writing or applicable law requires a
      different result.”</em>
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      “Can never be transferred” is usually too absolute. A later voluntary sale, refinance, gift, creditor lien,
      estate rule, court order, statutory right, or signed amendment may change the result. The goal is to define
      ownership clearly enough that a later dispute starts with evidence instead of ambiguity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it will not do</h2>
    <ul class="points">
      <li>It is not a law firm, and no document it produces has legal effect until independent counsel redrafts it and it is signed properly.</li>
      <li>It will not help you conceal an asset, backdate an instrument, or move property to defeat a creditor. Those destroy the plan and are the fastest route to losing the agreement.</li>
      <li>It will not pretend to know a jurisdiction it has not checked. Every forum is covered so none is silently missing, and the ones whose specific rules are unverified say so, in the documents, where you will read it.</li>
      <li>It cannot waive child support, decide custody, waive a retirement-plan survivor benefit before the wedding, or cancel an immigration affidavit of support. <a href="/limits/">Those limits are real and they are listed.</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How the drafts are grounded</h2>
    <p>
      The interview matches what you type against jurisdiction packs covering all fifty US states,
      the District of Columbia, and England &amp; Wales, Scotland, Northern Ireland, Ireland, Canada,
      Australia, New Zealand and Peru, plus a civil-law fallback. Each pack carries its property
      regime, its execution formalities — witnesses, acknowledgment form, waiting periods, whether
      independent counsel is a condition of validity — and the statutes or cases behind them.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">Free during MVP. Paid advisor is reserved for later.</p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
